                <pb facs="0248" n="218" sameAs="frus:frus1958-60v14/pg218"/>

                <head>69. Editorial Note</head>
                <p>On August 13, 1960, the Central African Republic became independent. For text of
                    a message of that date from President Eisenhower to Prime Minister David Dacko,
                    see <hi rendition="#italic">Public Papers of the Presidents of the United
                        States: Dwight D. Eisenhower, 1960–61</hi>, pages 630–631. Consul General at
                    Brazzaville Alan W. Lukens presented his credentials as Chargé d’Affaires on
                    August 13; see Department of State <hi rendition="#italic">Bulletin</hi>,
                    October 31, 1960, page 702. Lukens was resident at Brazzaville, as was the first
                    Ambassador to the Central African Republic, W. Wendell Blancké, who presented
                    his credentials on January 6, 1961.</p>
            </div>
